arm64: dts: mediatek: gemini: name the rail the A72 bring-up moves the CCI onto

`cci-supply` on cpu8 is not a standard property and is not that CPU's own
supply. It is the rail the CCI sits on, named here because cpu8 is where the
driver that moves the CCI lives and this SoC has no node for the interconnect
itself — inventing one for a device with no driver would be more fiction, not
less.

What it is for is in soc/0011 and in the comment: onlining cpu8 reprograms
ARMCAXPLL2, which is the CCI's PLL, from 630 to 750 MHz, and VPROC1's 1000 mV
floor was chosen assuming 630. Issue #84.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
@@ -3164,6 +3164,21 @@
 &cpu8 {
 	operating-points-v2 = <&cpu_opp_b>;
 	proc-supply = <&da9214_vproc2>;
+
+	/*
+	 * NOT a standard property, and not this CPU's own supply. It is the rail
+	 * the CCI sits on, named here because cpu8 is where the driver that
+	 * moves the CCI lives and there is no node for the interconnect itself.
+	 *
+	 * Onlining this core reprograms ARMCAXPLL2 — the CCI's PLL, not the A72
+	 * cluster's — from the 630 MHz the bootloader chose to 750 MHz, as a
+	 * prerequisite for the firmware's CPU_ON gate. The CCI shares VPROC1
+	 * with the eight A53s, whose tables floor at 1000 mV on the strength of
+	 * the CCI being at 630; the vendor prices 754 MHz at 1070 mV.
+	 * mt6797-a72.c claims this and holds a 1070 mV floor for as long as the
+	 * cluster is up. Issue #84.
+	 */
+	cci-supply = <&da9214_vproc>;
 };
 
 &cpu9 {
